ann2html turns the words you look up on your Kindle into Anki cards that look
exactly like the ones Yomitan makes.

Move this folder wherever you want; nothing needs to be installed. The app
keeps its data (imported dictionaries and settings) in ann2html.db next to
the executable.

Before you start
  * Anki must be running with the AnkiConnect add-on installed.
  * In Yomitan, export your settings (Settings > Backup > Export Settings) and
    your dictionaries (Settings > Backup > Export Dictionary Collection). You
    can also import individual dictionary .zip files instead.

Using it
  1. Run ./ann2html
  2. Select your vocab.db. It is under MOUNTPOINT/system/vocabulary/vocab.db
  3. Import your Yomitan settings and dictionaries (only needed once).
  4. Click "Mine cards". Only lookups made since the last run are used, and
     words that are already in Anki are skipped.
You can also drag and drop any of these files onto the window.

The optional ann script mounts your Kindle, opens ann2html and unmounts the
Kindle when you close it. It needs udisksctl. Add your Kindle's UUID to it:

To find your uuid run ls -l /dev/disk/by-uuid and then connect your kindle and run it again.
ls -l /dev/disk/by-uuid
total 0
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 10 Nov 10 00:00 3472-5482 -> ../../nvme
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 10 Nov 10 00:00 3472-54821212 -> ../../nvme2
PLUG IN KINDLE AND RUN AGAIN
ls -l /dev/disk/by-uuid
total 0
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 10 Nov 10 00:00 3472-5482 -> ../../nvme
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 10 Nov 10 00:00 3251-54821212 -> ../../nvme2
lrwxrwxrwx 1 root root 10 Nov 10 00:00 3582-6578 -> ../../sda
in this example the UUID you want is 3582-6578
Then in the script called ann add your kindle uuid to the following line.

kindle_UUID=
kindle_UUID=3582-6578

https://github.com/xythh/ann2html if you have issues or would like further instructions.
